This folder contains a replicated A/B comparison of two builds of Simu5G:
whether a change moves the distributions of the simulation results, and
which statistics it moves.

Why it is needed
----------------

A change that re-deals the random draws -- a draw added or removed, channel
state keyed per link instead of per model -- moves nearly every fingerprint
and every run-0 baseline, whether or not the model changed. One run per
version cannot tell a perturbation from a change of the model. Many runs per
version can: a perturbation leaves each statistic's distribution where it
was, a change of the model moves some of them.

How it works
------------

run.sh runs each configuration 30 times per side (seed sets 0..29) at two
Simu5G roots, A and B -- typically two git worktrees, each built in debug
mode -- and a third time at A with other seed sets (30..59): the null
control, A2.

compare.py then compares two sides statistic by statistic: for every scalar,
and for the count, mean, sum, min and max of every statistic, a two-sided
Mann-Whitney U test of the 30 values against the 30 of the other side, and the
Benjamini-Hochberg correction over the tests of a configuration (false
discovery rate 5%). It prints per configuration how many statistics were
tested, identical or skipped, how many have p < 0.05 before correction, and
the significant ones after it, with the effect size in pooled standard
deviations.

    ./run.sh /path/to/rootA /path/to/rootB configs.txt out
    ./compare.py out A B      # the change
    ./compare.py out A A2     # the null control

configs.txt has one "<dir>|<config>|<sim-time-limit>" line per configuration,
for example

    simulations/channelmodel|UMa-38901|20s
    simulations/nr/standalone|VoIP-DL|5s

Choose the configurations the change moves (the fingerprint suite says which)
and the ones that exercise what it touches; keep the simulated time short
enough for 90 runs of each.

Reading the result
------------------

 - The null control has to come out clean: no significant statistic in any
   configuration. If it does not, the configuration is too noisy for 30 runs
   or a statistic depends on more than the seed, and its A/B result means
   nothing.

 - With a clean control, a significant statistic is a change of the model, to
   be explained: by the change's purpose, or narrowed down to the commit that
   causes it by running the same configurations at the commits in between.

 - Identical statistics are not evidence that a change does nothing to them:
   a configuration whose results do not depend on what changed shows none.

Requirements: python3 with numpy and scipy.
